==Evolution==
Evolves into '''Guardian Gamereon''' at level 10.

Evolves into '''King Gamereon''' at level 30 using [[Catfruit]] and XP.

==Strategy/Usage==
Like the other ULTRA SOULS, Urashima Taro has significantly different stats between Normal (spammable) and Evolved Form (high performance), both of which can be used effectively depending on the situation.

==='''Normal Form'''===
Urashima Taro has a whole lot of range, but it's too much be used as a proper meatshield, so he ends up being more of a generalist attacker who doesn't die immediately once he is reached by the traits he targets. As for quality, he strongly resembles [[Figure Skating Cats (Super Rare Cat)|Drama Cats]], just with shorter range and better stats. Use him with meatshields to take out dangerous enemies with medium-short range.

==='''Evolved Form'''===
Guardian Gamereon is a tank with [[Resistant|Resistance]] to Dark and Angels, as well as very high health. His range means he only serves as a secondary line of defense, being unable to protect your lower-ranged units. Even against his target traits, he doesn't work all that well because Darks and Angels tend to spam melee pushers like [[Shadow Boxer K]] and [[Angelic Gory]], so he's better off against ranged threats that can't quickly get rid of him so they can't advance while the rest of your units catch up, as in the case with [[Director Kurosawah]] and [[Sunfish Jones]]. Unfortunately, accessible superior options already exist for these threats as well, so Gamereon is at best mediocre and cannot be relied on much.

==='''True Form'''===
King Gamereon doesn't change much. The extra stats do improve his impact on the field and he can outrange more threats, but he is generally unchanged for the most part, being a ranged tank that occasionally does a fair bit of damage. The [[Immune to Weaken|Weaken Immunity]] does sound good on paper, but not many enemies in his targeted traits utilize this ability in the first place, so it remains situational. He does get to shine more on stages where specialists like him are needed, but Curse effects and other strong threats outside of his target traits are all what's needed to remove his effectiveness. Nevertheless, despite not fixing any weaknesses that plagued this unit, this True Form should not be discounted.

==='''Talents'''===
'''[[Special Abilities#Resist|Resistance]] to [[Freeze]]''' and '''[[Knockback]]''' are not very useful in general. Darks don't rely on abilities much, making it pointless, and while [[Angelic Sleipnir]] does abuse them a lot, Gameron still isn't the best choice for stalling them in the first place to him. Likewise, '''[[Slow]]''' is not all that important. While Gameron does have a decently fast attack rate and can keep the melee pushers locked behind, its bad uptime means this job is better left to dedicated crowd controllers instead.

'''Defense Buff''' and '''Attack Buff''' are definitely important. Although Gameron may not be the most impressive generalist, these Talents do become significant because Gameron primarily relies on his high stats, specifically health, and more of either is always good. It's recommended to boost him with [[Catseye]]s before investing Talents for maximum effect as they cost a bit too much for the percentage they add.

==='''Ultra Talents'''===
For starters, Gamereon gained '''Cost Down'''. Gamereon wasn't very expensive to begin with, so this Talent feels like a minor improvement.

'''[[Immune to Slow|Slow Immune]]''' is a fine Talent. With it, Gamereon gains better matchups against Sunfish Jones, [[Cruel Angel Clionel]], [[Great Angel Chibinel]] and most importantly [[Tarsiriel]]. With this Talent, Gamereon is also safe against enemies like [[Croakley]]. This isn't a bad talent, but it's an Ultra Talent meaning that the potential viability of this Talent goes straight into the ground.

'''[[Curse]]''' would've been cool, with it lasting a whole six seconds, but it only has a 50% activation rate. This turns what would've been a very useful Talent into something painful to use or to rely on.

Overall, these Ultra Talents are not good for its cost. The only real benefit you get from getting Gamereon to level 60, besides the obvious stat increase, is Gamereon's second Orb slot. With two Orb slots, you can either double down on reduced damage against one trait, or gain a health increase on both of Gamereon's target traits instead of having to decide between one or the other.

==='''Orb'''===
The best Orb choice is Resist VS. Angel or Dark. Once Ultra Talented, you can choose to either double up on one trait or pick both. Doubling up is recommended due to the way the buffs are stacked, leading to an exponentially bigger gain. Knockback Resist A is a worthwhile option to give King Gamereon full Knockback Immunity with level 10 Knockback Resist Talent.

